 # Appearance Matching
-
-
 ## Overview
-Appearance matching is a post-training optimization procedure used in multi-block neural radiance field frameworks to ensure visual consistency across block boundaries [S1]. It solves the problem of appearance discontinuities that arise when independent Block-NeRFs are trained with randomly initialized appearance latent codes, which yield different colors for the same semantic code in adjacent blocks [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard training by freezing network weights and optimizing only latent codes or specific appearance parameters, whereas siblings like [HybridNeRF Model Architecture](4842-hybridnerf-model-architecture.md) or [VoxNeRF](8865-voxnerf.md) integrate appearance handling into the primary forward pass without separate consistency post-processing [S1].
